Verification at the Speed of Intelligence: A Formal Framework for Managing Capability–Verification–Institution Dynamics in Rapidly Advancing AI Systems

Ouadi Maakoul
Independent Researcher
For Humanity 4

September 2026

---

Abstract

Recent developments in artificial intelligence have created a novel systems problem: machine systems are performing increasingly sophisticated mathematical reasoning, scientific discovery, and technical problem solving at rates that may exceed the adaptive capacity of existing human verification and governance institutions. The September 2026 declaration by twenty-five Fields Medalists, responding to OpenAI’s Navier–Stokes claim, provides a concrete case study of this phenomenon.

This paper develops a formal framework for analyzing the relationship between machine capability C_i(t), verification capacity V_i(t), institutional adaptation capacity I_i(t), and effective human control H_i(t), domain-indexed by i. We define the Capability–Verification Gap G_{CV,i}(t) and the Capability–Institution Gap G_{CI,i}(t), introduce the Verification Acceleration Differential A_{CV,i}(t), and propose verification debt B_{V,i}(t) as an accumulated liability with explicit repayment dynamics. We correct the reliability model for verification cascades to account for correlated failures across redundant layers using an effective-number-of-layers formulation, and we address the recursive verification problem through a verification depth formula with an effective-independence discount. Deployment thresholds are made stateful, gated on both current verification capacity and outstanding verification debt.

We apply the framework quantitatively to the Navier–Stokes episode, demonstrating that OpenAI’s decision not to claim the Millennium Prize is consistent with a positive Capability–Institution Gap and with the Clay Institute’s procedural requirements—a structural constraint that would apply regardless of OpenAI’s internal confidence. We position the framework against adjacent literatures—scalable oversight, Responsible Scaling Policies (including the significant shift in Anthropic’s RSP v3.0), AI control research, and differential technological development—and identify recursive verification and Goodhart effects as critical failure modes. We conclude with a falsifiable research agenda and a companion computational proposal.

---

1. Introduction

Artificial intelligence has historically been evaluated through capability benchmarks: mathematics, coding, scientific reasoning, planning, multimodal perception, autonomous tool use. However, capability is only one component of a functioning socio-technical system. A civilization deploying increasingly capable systems also requires verification (determining whether outputs are correct), interpretability (understanding relevant mechanisms), monitoring (detecting anomalous behavior), institutional adaptation (updating rules and procedures), human authority (retaining meaningful decision rights), and containment (limiting consequences when verification fails).

The events of September 2026 provide a concrete motivating case. On September 8, OpenAI announced that an internal model had solved the Navier–Stokes Millennium Prize problem. The announcement was met with immediate skepticism because the proof had not been released for independent verification. On September 11, twenty-five Fields Medalists—including Terence Tao, Peter Scholze, Maryna Viazovska, and 2026 medalist Yu Deng—published a declaration titled “A Severe Misalignment of AI in Mathematics,” warning that the push by AI companies to solve mathematical problems as benchmarks is detrimental to the science of mathematics and that the goals of AI companies and the mathematical community are severely misaligned.

OpenAI subsequently published a write-up, PDF paper, and Lean formalization, while stating it did not intend to claim the Millennium Prize. The proof was produced by approximately 10,000 concurrent agents working across about 88 hours, consuming 2.7 million messages and around 130 billion output tokens, with Lean formalization taking an additional 17 hours using GPT-6 Astra.

This episode illustrates the central problem: a capability claim was made faster than the verification community could assess it. The gap between the claim and its verification created uncertainty that institutions could not resolve at the speed at which the claim was made.

This paper asks a systems question: can human verification, oversight, and institutional adaptation scale at least as rapidly as machine capability? We develop a formal framework, provide operational definitions, and propose a research agenda.

