2. Formal Framework

2.1 State Variables

We define a domain-indexed state vector. For each domain i \in \{1, \ldots, n\} (e.g., mathematics, biology, cybersecurity, law):

\mathbf{x}_i(t) = \big(C_i(t), V_i(t), I_i(t), H_i(t), K_i(t), D_i(t)\big),

where C_i(t) is machine capability, V_i(t) is verification capacity, I_i(t) is institutional adaptation capacity, H_i(t) is effective human control, K_i(t) is containment/reversibility, and D_i(t) is deployment scale. This domain-indexed formulation replaces a single scalar C(t) and prevents the aggregation error of comparing incommensurable domains.

2.2 Operational Definitions

Capability C_i(t): the rate at which system i produces outputs that meet a domain-specific difficulty threshold. For mathematics, this may be the rate of theorems produced that survive formal verification. For biology, the rate of experimentally validated hypotheses.

Verification capacity V_i(t): defined as

V_i(t) = \tau_i(t) \cdot \kappa_i(t) \cdot R_{\text{sys},i}(t),

where \tau_i is verification throughput (proofs checked per unit time), \kappa_i is coverage (fraction of outputs for which verification is attempted), and R_{\text{sys},i} is system-level verification reliability. Independence enters through the correlation structure of R_{\text{sys},i}, not as a separate multiplicative factor.

Institutional adaptation capacity I_i(t): defined as

I_i(t) = \frac{\sigma_i(t) \ln 2}{T_i^{\text{adapt}}(t)},

where \sigma_i is the scope of institutional change (fraction of relevant rules modified) and T_i^{\text{adapt}} is the adaptation half-life (time for a regulatory change to take effect). The \ln 2 factor ensures dimensional consistency with the half-life interpretation. This captures the observation that an institution may understand a technology is changing while remaining unable to modify regulations, standards, or infrastructure quickly enough.

Effective human control H_i(t): a relational variable, not a scalar. Meaningful control requires sufficient information, time, authority to intervene, understanding of consequences, and practical ability to reject the system’s recommendation. We define

H_i(t) = \min\big(H_i^{\text{info}}, H_i^{\text{time}}, H_i^{\text{authority}}, H_i^{\text{contestability}}\big),

where each component is measured on [0,1]. Human presence does not imply human control. The \min operator captures the fact that these components are not perfectly substitutable: a human with perfect information but no time cannot exercise control. For graded compensation, one may replace \min with a soft-min or an L_p norm with p<0.

2.3 Gap Definitions

We use a single, floored logarithmic convention for the Capability–Verification Gap:

G_{CV,i}(t) = \ln\left(\frac{C_i(t) + \epsilon_C}{V_i(t) + \epsilon_V}\right),

where \epsilon_C, \epsilon_V > 0 are small floors preventing division by zero and ensuring the gap is well-defined even when V_i = 0. This replaces the earlier inconsistent dual definition (1 - V/C and \ln(C/V) are not equivalent) and the ill-defined \ln V_i at announcement time.

The Verification Acceleration Differential:

A_{CV,i}(t) = \frac{d\ln(C_i + \epsilon_C)}{dt} - \frac{d\ln(V_i + \epsilon_V)}{dt}.

When A_{CV,i}(t) > 0, capability is increasing faster than verification capacity. This does not prove danger; it identifies a condition requiring measurement. The floors ensure A_{CV} is defined even at t_0 when V_i \approx 0.

The Capability–Institution Gap:

G_{CI,i}(t) = \ln\left(\frac{C_i(t) + \epsilon_C}{I_i(t) + \epsilon_I}\right),

with adaptation differential

A_{CI,i}(t) = \frac{d\ln(C_i + \epsilon_C)}{dt} - \frac{d\ln(I_i + \epsilon_I)}{dt}.

2.4 Verification Debt with Repayment Dynamics

We introduce verification debt as an accumulated liability with both accumulation and repayment:

B_{V,i}(t) = \int_{t_0}^{t} \max\big(0, C_i(s) - V_i(s)\big) \cdot e^{-\lambda(t-s)} \, ds - \int_{t_0}^{t} V_i^{\text{retro}}(s) \, ds,

where \lambda is a depreciation rate for unverified claims (accounting for obsolescence) and V_i^{\text{retro}}(s) is verification effort applied to historical outputs. This allows debt to decrease when past capability is retrospectively verified. Verification debt represents the total quantity of unverified or under-verified capability that has accumulated. Debt must be repaid before deployment can expand safely. This is analogous to technical debt in software engineering: it does not cause immediate failure, but it increases the probability of failure under stress.

