4. The Verification Cascade

4.1 Architecture

For increasingly capable systems, verification itself may need multiple independent layers. We propose:

Level Method Domain examples
V_0 Output inspection Human reads the answer
V_1 Independent model verification Second AI evaluates the result
V_2 Formal verification Lean, Coq, Isabelle
V_3 Experimental verification Laboratory test
V_4 Independent replication Independent laboratory
V_5 Adversarial verification Red-teaming for counterexamples
V_6 Incentive verification Does the verifier have incentive to tell the truth?

Different domains require different verification layers. A mathematical theorem may rely heavily on V_2. A new drug may require V_2 + V_3 + V_4. A cybersecurity system may require extensive V_5. V_6 is relevant wherever the verifier’s interests may diverge from truth-telling—including corporate self-verification, as illustrated by the RSP v3.0 retreat discussed in §6.2.

4.2 Reliability Model with Correlated Failures

A critical error in earlier formulations was the claim that R_{\text{system}} \approx \min_i R_i, which assumes a series architecture. The verification cascade is better modeled as a hybrid of mandatory and redundant layers, with correlated failures.

For a series chain (all stages must pass):

R_{\text{series}} = \prod_{i=1}^{n} R_i.

For a parallel/redundant system with independent layers:

R_{\text{parallel}} = 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{n} (1 - R_i).

However, if verification layers are correlated—e.g., built by the same organization, using the same model family, or trained on the same data—the effective reliability is lower. We model this with an effective-number-of-layers correction. If all redundant layers have the same reliability R, define

n_{\text{eff}} = \frac{n}{1 + \rho(n-1)},

so that

R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}} = 1 - (1 - R)^{n_{\text{eff}}}.

When \rho = 0, we recover the independent case (n_{\text{eff}} = n). When \rho = 1, the redundant layers collapse to a single layer (n_{\text{eff}} = 1). When \rho \to \infty, adding more layers provides no benefit. This corrects the earlier formula 1 - \prod (1 - R_i)^{1/(1+\rho)}, which incorrectly drove reliability to zero as \rho \to \infty.

The correct model for a verification cascade is therefore hybrid:

R_{\text{system}} = R_{\text{mandatory}} \cdot \left(1 - (1 - R_{\text{redundant}})^{n_{\text{eff}}}\right),

where R_{\text{mandatory}} = \prod_{i \in \text{mandatory}} R_i.

Verification Bottleneck Principle: The practical safety of a high-capability socio-technical system cannot exceed the reliability of its weakest mandatory verification layer, but redundant layers can partially compensate—subject to their effective independence.

Estimating \rho: The correlation parameter \rho is not directly observable but can be proxied by: (i) shared training data (Jaccard similarity of training corpora), (ii) organizational overlap (same lab, same funding source, same personnel), (iii) model family distance (same base model, same architecture, same alignment procedure), and (iv) shared failure modes (do the layers fail on the same inputs?). A toy calibration for the Navier–Stokes case: V_1 (GPT-6 Astra self-verification by OpenAI) has \rho \approx 0.8; V_2 (Lean formalization) has \rho \approx 0.1 relative to V_1 but \rho \approx 0.9 relative to the unformalized mathematical claim if the formalization uses a forced variant; V_4 (independent replication) has \rho \approx 0. These proxies make RQ11 (recursive verification) empirically testable.

