6. Institutional Adaptation as a Dynamical System

6.1 Adaptive Governance

Traditional regulation tends to operate through discrete updates. But rapidly changing technology may require continuous adaptation. We therefore propose:

\frac{dR}{dt} = g(C, V, I, E),

where institutional rules become an adaptive dynamical system. An adaptive institution continuously monitors C(t), V(t), I(t), E(t) and modifies policies when defined thresholds are crossed. This resembles a control system:

\text{Measure} \to \text{Compare} \to \text{Decide} \to \text{Act} \to \text{Measure again}.

Governance becomes an engineering feedback loop. The literature on complex systems governance supports this: governance mechanisms should be “feedback-driven processes that iteratively respond and adapt to new information while preserving overarching societal goals and values”. Regulatory sandboxes (FDA’s TEMPO pilot, MHRA’s AI Airlock) provide concrete implementations of adaptive oversight.

6.2 Related Institutional Models: The Case of RSP v3.0

The framework’s G_{CI} concept is well illustrated by the recent evolution of Anthropic’s Responsible Scaling Policy. The original RSP defined capability levels and corresponding mitigations, with a commitment to pause development and deployment if the company couldn’t implement required mitigations before reaching the next level. The February 2026 rewrite (RSP v3.0) removed that commitment, replacing it with a discretionary, competitor-conditioned pause. GovAI’s initial reaction was “rather negative” precisely because removal of the pause made deploying risky models more likely.

This is a textbook example of G_{CI}: institutional commitment retreating under competitive pressure even as capability accelerates. The pause commitment was a deployment threshold; its removal represents I_i(t) decreasing, not increasing. RSP v3.0 introduced Risk Reports and a Frontier Safety Roadmap as compensating transparency mechanisms, but these are non-binding and self-reported. The policy has since moved to v3.2 (April 29, 2026), two revisions beyond the version originally cited.

6.3 Goodhart Risk

Once G_{CV,i} becomes an official gating metric, there is pressure to optimize the proxy for verification rather than verification itself. We must anticipate this failure mode. Mitigations include: multiple independent verification metrics, adversarial audits of the verification process itself, and periodic revalidation of the relationship between the proxy and the underlying property.

---

7. Deployment Thresholds

Some domains may not permit rapid verification. In such cases, deployment should be conditional on verification capacity and verification debt:

\text{Deploy}_i(t) =
\begin{cases}
1, & V_i(t) \geq V_{\min,i}(C_i, \mathcal{A}_i, B_{V,i}) \text{ and } H_i(t) \geq H_{\min,i} \text{ and } K_i(t) \geq K_{\min,i}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

The minimum verification threshold V_{\min,i} depends on outstanding verification debt B_{V,i}: even if current V is high, a large unpaid debt should gate deployment. This aligns with the debt metaphor: you cannot take new loans while old debt is outstanding. This produces the general design principle:

\text{Capability expansion} \Rightarrow \text{Verification expansion} \Rightarrow \text{Deployment expansion}

not

\text{Capability expansion} \Rightarrow \text{automatic deployment}.

This is a normative principle, not a derived theorem.

