8. Worked Example: The Navier–Stokes Episode (Quantitative with Uncertainty)

8.1 Capability Claim

On September 8, OpenAI announced that an internal model had solved the Navier–Stokes Millennium Prize problem. The model ran approximately 10,000 concurrent agents across 88 hours (September 1–5), consuming 2.7 million messages and around 130 billion output tokens. The proof was subsequently formalized in Lean, with formalization and verification taking an additional 17 hours using GPT-6 Astra.

8.2 Quantified Verification Assessment

Layer Status Estimated parameters Notes
V_0 ❌ Proof not released initially \kappa \approx 0 
V_1 ⚠️ GPT-6 Astra checked R_1 \approx 0.95, \rho \approx 0.8 Same organization as C
V_2 ⚠️ Lean formalization published R_2 \approx 0.99 for formalized statement Forced-variant issue
V_3 N/A  Mathematical result
V_4 ❌ Not yet achieved R_4 \approx 0 Not attempted
V_5 ⚠️ Community skepticism R_5 \approx 0.3 No formal refutation
V_6 ⚠️ Incentive misalignment  Corporate self-verification

A critical factor for V_2 is statement-match:

V_2^{\text{statement-match}} = \mathbb{I}[\text{formal} \equiv \text{Clay}].

OpenAI’s result establishes statement ‘C’ (and also ‘D’) in the official Millennium Prize formulation, but the proof uses a forced variant—a smooth force applied throughout. Whether a blowup produced under an applied force satisfies the Clay Institute’s unforced formulation is the crux. If the formalization covers only the forced variant, then V_2^{\text{statement-match}} = 0 for the Clay claim, and R_2 \approx 0.99 applies only to the formalized statement, not to the prize claim.

8.3 Gap Analysis with Uncertainty Bars

At the time of announcement:

· Capability rate C_i: the system produced a claimed proof of a Millennium Problem in 88 hours. For a rough normalization, a human mathematician might take years to decades. If we set C_i = 1 proof per 88 hours, and a human benchmark at 10^{-3} proofs per 88 hours, then C_i / C_i^{\text{human}} \approx 1000.
· Verification rate V_i: at announcement, \kappa \approx 0 (no independent verification attempted), so V_i \approx 0.
· Gap: G_{CV,i} = \ln((C_i + \epsilon_C)/(V_i + \epsilon_V)) \approx \ln(1/\epsilon_V) (maximum gap).
· Acceleration differential: A_{CV,i} \gg 0. Capability spiked from near-zero to a claimed Millennium solution in days. Verification capacity remained approximately constant.

After the Lean formalization was published 17 hours later:

· V_i = \tau_i \cdot \kappa_i \cdot R_{\text{sys},i}. If \tau_i \approx 1 proof per 17 hours and \kappa_i \approx 0.3 (limited by the forced-variant issue), then V_i \approx 0.3/17 proofs per hour \approx 0.018 proofs per hour, compared to C_i \approx 1/88 \approx 0.011 proofs per hour. This would make V_i > C_i superficially, but the effective gap remains positive because V_4 and V_5 are incomplete and V_2^{\text{statement-match}} = 0 for the Clay claim.
· The gap narrows but remains positive: G_{CV,i} \approx 0.7–0.9 (with uncertainty \pm 0.1), because independent replication (V_4) and adversarial verification (V_5) remain incomplete.

8.4 Institutional Response and the Clay Institute Constraint

The Fields Medalists’ declaration represents an institutional adaptation (I) that occurred after the capability claim, not before. The G_{CI} was therefore positive during the critical period.

OpenAI’s decision not to claim the Millennium Prize is not primarily a signal about its internal assessment of the verification gap. Under Clay Mathematics Institute rules, a proposed solution must satisfy three conditions before CMI will even consider it:

1. Published in a Qualifying Outlet.
2. At least two years must have passed since publication.
3. The solution must have received general acceptance in the global mathematics community.

This is a procedural constraint that would apply regardless of how confident OpenAI was in the proof. The Clay rules themselves are an institutional mechanism (I) that cannot move at capability speed. An 88-hour proof cannot satisfy a two-year waiting period. This is actually a cleaner illustration of G_{CI} than it is evidence about OpenAI’s internal verification assessment: the institution’s rules are structurally slower than the capability.

8.5 Verification Debt Accumulation

The verification debt B_V accumulated during the period between the announcement (September 8) and the publication of the Lean formalization (September 9). Using the debt formula:

B_V(t) = \int_{t_0}^{t} \max(0, C_i(s) - V_i(s)) \cdot e^{-\lambda(t-s)} \, ds - \int V_i^{\text{retro}}(s) \, ds,

during the ~17-hour window where V_i \approx 0 and C_i \approx 1 (normalized proof), the debt accumulated at rate \approx 1 proof-equivalent per 17 hours. The subsequent Lean formalization provided partial repayment, but V_4 (independent replication) and V_5 (adversarial verification) remain incomplete, so the debt is not fully repaid. The repayment window should be defined ex ante (e.g., 30 days for mathematical claims) to avoid post-hoc fitting in empirical tests.

8.6 Lessons

1. Capability claims can be made faster than verification can be completed.
2. The absence of verification creates uncertainty that institutions must resolve.
3. Formal verification (V_2) can reduce the gap, but only if it is independent and accessible, and it may not cover the full claim (the forced-variant issue).
4. Institutional responses (I) may lag behind capability claims—and in the Clay case, they are structurally constrained to do so.
5. The decision not to claim a prize may reflect procedural constraints as much as internal assessment of verification.

