9. Related Work

9.1 Scalable Oversight and Weak-to-Strong Generalization

Scalable oversight seeks to augment human supervision with AI assistants when models surpass human performance. Weak-to-strong generalization (W2SG) studies whether weaker supervisors can elicit correct behavior from stronger models. This is directly relevant to V_1: if weaker models cannot reliably verify stronger models, then AI-assisted verification may fail. Recent work shows that weak verifiers achieve high accuracy on correct reasoning but fail to detect 20–40% of deceptive reasoning. Our framework suggests that V_1 reliability decreases as C/V increases, and that the effective independence \rho between V_1 and the system being verified is a key parameter.

9.2 Responsible Scaling Policies (RSPs)

Anthropic’s RSP v3.0 removed the pause commitment that defined the original RSP, replacing it with Risk Reports and a non-binding Frontier Safety Roadmap. This is structurally similar to our deployment threshold D_i(t), but inverted: it illustrates I_i(t) decreasing under competitive pressure, not a working threshold.

9.3 AI Control Research

AI control research focuses on practical techniques—restricting capabilities, using oversight models, red-teaming—to prevent catastrophic outcomes from misaligned systems. Control evaluations test whether safety measures hold when the model may try to subvert them. This corresponds to our V_5 (adversarial verification) layer. Recent red-teaming work shows that untrusted models can achieve 6% attack success rates at 1% false-positive rates, illustrating the practical difficulty of adversarial verification.

9.4 Differential Technological Development

Bostrom’s differential technological development proposes influencing the sequence in which technologies develop: retarding harmful technologies while accelerating beneficial ones. Our framework operationalizes this idea by providing measurable quantities (G_{CV}, A_{CV}, B_V) that can guide sequencing decisions.

9.5 Adaptive Regulation

Recent work on complex systems governance proposes early and scalable intervention, adaptive institutional design, and risk thresholds calibrated to nonlinearity. Regulatory sandboxes (FDA TEMPO, MHRA AI Airlock) provide concrete implementations of adaptive oversight. Our I_i(t) and A_{CI,i}(t) provide quantitative measures for evaluating whether these mechanisms are keeping pace.

