10. Research Agenda

RQ1. Can verification capacity V_i(t) be quantitatively measured across technical domains?

RQ2. Does verification capability scale with AI capability? Under what conditions does A_{CV,i}(t) \leq 0?

RQ3. Which verification methods scale superlinearly, linearly, or sublinearly with capability?

RQ4. What constitutes a verification bottleneck in hybrid series-parallel architectures with correlated failures? How should \rho be estimated in practice?

RQ5. Can independent AI systems reliably verify increasingly capable AI systems? What is the relationship between C/V and V_1 reliability?

RQ6. When does formal verification become practical for high-consequence domains?

RQ7. How rapidly can institutions adapt to technological capability changes? What is the distribution of T_i^{\text{adapt}} across domains?

RQ8. Can adaptive governance reduce the capability–institution gap? What is the optimal control policy for R(t)?

RQ9. What minimum verification threshold V_{\min,i} should be required before deployment in high-consequence domains? How should V_{\min,i} depend on B_{V,i}?

RQ10. How does verification debt B_V accumulate and what are its consequences? What repayment windows are appropriate?

RQ11. What are the failure modes of recursive verification (V_1) and how can independence be ensured? How can V_6 (incentive verification) be operationalized?

RQ12. How can Goodhart effects on G_{CV} be detected and mitigated?

---

11. Falsifiability

The framework should be capable of being wrong. We propose testable hypotheses:

H1. Domains with A_{CV} > 0 will exhibit higher rates of propagated errors, controlling for C, D, and K.

H2. Domains possessing scalable formal verification will exhibit smaller effective verification gaps than domains relying on informal verification.

H3. Institutions with continuous monitoring and adaptive procedures will exhibit lower G_{CI} than institutions relying exclusively on periodic regulation.

H4. Independent verification architectures (V_4, V_5) will reduce the probability that incorrect AI-generated results propagate into consequential systems.

H5. Capability growth alone will be an insufficient predictor of system-level risk once V, I, H, \mathcal{A}, D, K are incorporated.

H6. Verification debt B_V will predict future failure rates better than current capability C alone, with a specified repayment window to avoid post-hoc fitting.

Each hypothesis can be empirically evaluated through longitudinal studies of AI deployment in specific domains. A companion computational notebook computing G_{CV}, A_{CV}, and B_V from toy traces (including the Navier–Stokes timeline) would make RQ1–RQ3 immediately actionable for other labs.

---

12. Conclusion

The concerns expressed by mathematicians in September 2026 deserve serious scientific examination—not because every prediction must be correct, but because rapid capability change creates a measurable systems problem.

The appropriate response is neither complacency nor speculation. It is measurement. Measure C_i(t), V_i(t), I_i(t), H_i(t), and continuously evaluate d\ln C_i/dt, d\ln V_i/dt, d\ln I_i/dt, d\ln H_i/dt. If machine capability accelerates faster than verification and institutional adaptation, the gap becomes an observable engineering quantity rather than an abstract fear.

The framework developed here provides operational definitions, a corrected reliability model for correlated verification cascades, a debt-based deployment threshold, and a falsifiable research agenda. The Navier–Stokes episode illustrates both the reality of the Capability–Verification Gap and the structural slowness of institutional adaptation: the Clay Institute’s two-year rule cannot be accelerated by any amount of AI capability.

The long-term challenge may be summarized by one principle:

\boxed{\textbf{Humanity must not only build intelligent machines.}}

\boxed{\textbf{Humanity must build institutions capable of verifying, governing, and cooperating with intelligence that evolves faster than itself.}}

That problem is not exclusively an AI problem. It is a civilization-design problem. And if humanity eventually becomes multiplanetary, the requirement becomes even stronger: intelligence scales across worlds, so verification and governance must scale across worlds.


