Appendix B. Gap Definitions: Equivalence Failure and Corrected Form

Proposition B.1 (Non-equivalence of gap definitions). The two candidate definitions

G^{(1)}(C,V) = 1 - \frac{V}{C}, \qquad G^{(2)}(C,V) = \ln\left(\frac{C}{V}\right)

are not equivalent on the domain C, V > 0.

Proof. Take C = 10, V = 5. Then G^{(1)} = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5 and G^{(2)} = \ln 2 \approx 0.693. Since 0.5 \neq 0.693, the two definitions differ. Moreover, G^{(1)} is bounded above by 1 (when V \to 0) but unbounded below (when V \gg C, G^{(1)} \to -\infty), whereas G^{(2)} is unbounded in both directions. ∎

Proposition B.2 (Domain failure of the log gap). G^{(2)}(C,V) = \ln(C/V) is undefined when V = 0, and G^{(2)} \to +\infty as V \to 0^+. At the announcement time of the Navier–Stokes episode, V \approx 0, so G^{(2)} is not finite.

Proof. Immediate from the definition of the logarithm. ∎

Definition B.3 (Floored log gap). The corrected Capability–Verification Gap is

G_{CV}(t) = \ln\left(\frac{C(t) + \epsilon_C}{V(t) + \epsilon_V}\right)

with \epsilon_C, \epsilon_V > 0. Similarly,

G_{CI}(t) = \ln\left(\frac{C(t) + \epsilon_C}{I(t) + \epsilon_I}\right).

Proposition B.4 (Properties of the floored log gap).

1. Well-definedness. G_{CV}(t) is defined for all C(t), V(t) \geq 0.
2. Monotonicity in C. \partial G_{CV}/\partial C = 1/(C + \epsilon_C) > 0.
3. Monotonicity in V. \partial G_{CV}/\partial V = -1/(V + \epsilon_V) < 0.
4. Sign. G_{CV} > 0 \iff C > V (assuming \epsilon_C = \epsilon_V).
5. Bound at zero verification. If V = 0, G_{CV} = \ln((C + \epsilon_C)/\epsilon_V), finite but large.

Proof. (1) Numerator and denominator are strictly positive. (2)–(3) Direct differentiation. (4) With \epsilon_C = \epsilon_V = \epsilon, G_{CV} = \ln((C+\epsilon)/(V+\epsilon)), which is positive iff C > V. (5) Substitute V = 0. ∎

Definition B.5 (Verification Acceleration Differential). The corrected acceleration differential is

A_{CV}(t) = \frac{d}{dt} \ln(C(t) + \epsilon_C) - \frac{d}{dt} \ln(V(t) + \epsilon_V).

Proposition B.6 (Sign interpretation). A_{CV}(t) > 0 if and only if the relative growth rate of capability (floored) exceeds the relative growth rate of verification (floored).

Proof. By definition of the logarithmic derivative. ∎

Remark B.7. At t_0 where V(t_0) = 0 but V is increasing, A_{CV} is finite and negative if \dot{V} > 0 is large enough. This is the desired behavior: the framework should register recovery from a zero-verification state, which the unf loored \ln V cannot.

