Appendix C. Correlated Verification Cascades: Derivation and Properties

C.1 The series architecture

Lemma C.1 (Series reliability). If a verification cascade requires all n mandatory layers to pass, and layer j has reliability R_j (probability of correctly passing a correct result), then the system reliability is

R_{\text{series}} = \prod_{j=1}^{n} R_j,

assuming the layers are stochastically independent in their pass/fail decisions.

Proof. The system passes iff every layer passes. Under independence, the probability of the intersection is the product. ∎

C.2 The parallel architecture with independent layers

Lemma C.2 (Independent parallel reliability). If a cascade accepts a result when at least one of n redundant layers passes it, and failures are independent,

R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{indep}} = 1 - \prod_{j=1}^{n} (1 - R_j).

Proof. The system fails iff every layer fails. By independence, P(\text{all fail}) = \prod (1-R_j), so R = 1 - \prod(1-R_j). ∎

C.3 Correlation and the effective number of layers

Definition C.3 (Equicorrelated failure indicators). For n redundant layers with identical reliability R, let F_j \in \{0,1\} indicate failure of layer j, with P(F_j = 1) = q = 1 - R. The failures are equicorrelated with parameter \rho \in [0,1] if

\operatorname{Corr}(F_j, F_k) = \rho \quad \text{for all } j \neq k.

Lemma C.4 (Variance of the failure sum under equicorrelation). Under equicorrelation,

\operatorname{Var}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n} F_j\right) = n q(1-q)\bigl(1 + (n-1)\rho\bigr).

Proof. Expand \operatorname{Var}(\sum F_j) = \sum_j \operatorname{Var}(F_j) + \sum_{j \neq k} \operatorname{Cov}(F_j, F_k). Each variance is q(1-q). Each covariance is \rho q(1-q). There are n variances and n(n-1) covariances. Thus

\operatorname{Var} = n q(1-q) + n(n-1) \rho q(1-q) = n q(1-q)(1 + (n-1)\rho).

∎

Definition C.5 (Effective number of layers). The effective number of layers is

n_{\text{eff}} = \frac{n}{1 + (n-1)\rho}.

This is the standard design-effect adjustment for clustered or correlated observations; it satisfies n_{\text{eff}} = \operatorname{Var}(\sum F_j) / \operatorname{Var}(\sum F_j \mid \rho = 0) \cdot n.

Proposition C.6 (Properties of n_{\text{eff}}).

1. n_{\text{eff}} \in [1, n] for all \rho \in [0,1].
2. n_{\text{eff}}(\rho = 0) = n.
3. n_{\text{eff}}(\rho = 1) = 1.
4. n_{\text{eff}} is strictly decreasing in \rho.
5. \lim_{n \to \infty} n_{\text{eff}} = 1/\rho for \rho > 0.
6. n_{\text{eff}} is strictly increasing in n.

Proof. (1) Since \rho \in [0,1], 1 + (n-1)\rho \in [1, n], so n_{\text{eff}} \in [1,n]. (2)–(3) Direct substitution. (4) \partial n_{\text{eff}}/\partial \rho = -n(n-1)/(1+(n-1)\rho)^2 < 0. (5) n_{\text{eff}} = n/(1+(n-1)\rho) \to 1/\rho as n \to \infty. (6) \partial n_{\text{eff}}/\partial n = (1-\rho)/(1+(n-1)\rho)^2 \geq 0 with equality only at \rho = 1. ∎

Definition C.7 (Effective parallel reliability). For equicorrelated redundant layers with common reliability R,

R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}} = 1 - (1-R)^{n_{\text{eff}}}.

Theorem C.8 (Limiting behavior of effective parallel reliability).

1. R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}}(\rho = 0) = 1 - (1-R)^n = R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{indep}}.
2. R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}}(\rho = 1) = R.
3. R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}} is strictly decreasing in \rho.
4. R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}} \in [R, 1-(1-R)^n] for all \rho \in [0,1].

Proof. (1) n_{\text{eff}}(\rho=0) = n. (2) n_{\text{eff}}(\rho=1) = 1, so R = 1-(1-R)^1. (3) Since 1-R \in [0,1] and n_{\text{eff}} is decreasing in \rho, the function \rho \mapsto (1-R)^{n_{\text{eff}}(\rho)} is increasing in \rho, so R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}} is decreasing. (4) From (1)–(3). ∎

Remark C.9 (Correction of the earlier formula). The previous draft proposed

R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}} = 1 - \prod_{j=1}^{n}(1-R_j)^{1/(1+\rho)}.

This is incorrect. As \rho \to \infty, the exponent 1/(1+\rho) \to 0, so each factor (1-R_j)^{1/(1+\rho)} \to 1, and the product \to 1, giving R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}} \to 0. That is, the formula predicts that perfectly correlated redundant layers have zero reliability — the opposite of the intended behavior. The correct limiting behavior for perfectly correlated layers is R_{\text{parallel}}^{\text{eff}} \to R, the reliability of a single layer.

C.4 The hybrid architecture

Definition C.10 (Hybrid reliability). If a cascade has a set \mathcal{M} of mandatory layers (all must pass) and a set \mathcal{R} of redundant layers (at least one must pass), with the two sets treated as independent of each other, then

R_{\text{system}} = \left(\prod_{j \in \mathcal{M}} R_j\right) \cdot \left(1 - (1 - \bar{R}_{\mathcal{R}})^{n_{\text{eff}}^{\mathcal{R}}}\right),

where \bar{R}_{\mathcal{R}} is the (geometric or arithmetic) mean reliability of the redundant layers and n_{\text{eff}}^{\mathcal{R}} is the effective number of redundant layers.

Proposition C.11 (Verification Bottleneck Principle). R_{\text{system}} \leq \min_{j \in \mathcal{M}} R_j, with equality only if the redundant block has reliability 1.

Proof. The mandatory product is \leq \min_j R_j since each R_j \in [0,1]. The redundant block factor is \leq 1. ∎

Remark C.12 (Estimating \rho). See Appendix N.

