Appendix D. Verification Depth: Full Derivation and Closed Form

D.1 Problem statement

We seek the minimum number n of verification layers such that the system-level error probability — the probability that an incorrect result passes all redundant layers — is at most \delta, given per-layer error probability \epsilon and equicorrelation \rho.

Definition D.1 (Target effective depth). Define

N^* = \log_{1/\epsilon}\!\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right) = \frac{\ln(1/\delta)}{\ln(1/\epsilon)}.

N^* is the number of independent layers required to reduce the error probability from \epsilon to \delta, since \epsilon^{N^*} = \delta.

D.2 Independent case

Lemma D.2 (Independent verification depth). If \rho = 0, the minimum n is

n_{\min} = \lceil N^* \rceil.

Proof. With independent layers, system error = \epsilon^n. We need \epsilon^n \leq \delta, i.e., n \ln \epsilon \leq \ln \delta, i.e., n \geq \ln(1/\delta)/\ln(1/\epsilon) = N^*. ∎

D.3 Correlated case

Theorem D.3 (Correlated verification depth). Suppose \rho \in [0,1] and \epsilon \in (0,1), \delta \in (0,1). Let N^* = \ln(1/\delta)/\ln(1/\epsilon).

1. If N^* \leq 1, then n_{\min} = 1.
2. If N^* > 1 and \rho = 0, then n_{\min} = \lceil N^* \rceil.
3. If N^* > 1 and 0 < \rho < 1/N^*, then

n_{\min} = \left\lceil \frac{N^*(1-\rho)}{1 - N^*\rho} \right\rceil.

4. If N^* > 1 and \rho \geq 1/N^*, no finite n suffices; decorrelation of the layers is required.

Proof. The system error under equicorrelated redundancy is modeled as \epsilon^{n_{\text{eff}}} where n_{\text{eff}} = n/(1+(n-1)\rho). We require

\epsilon^{n_{\text{eff}}} \leq \delta
\iff n_{\text{eff}} \ln \epsilon \leq \ln \delta
\iff n_{\text{eff}} \geq \frac{\ln(1/\delta)}{\ln(1/\epsilon)} = N^*.

So the problem reduces to finding the minimum n \geq 1 with n_{\text{eff}}(n) \geq N^*.

(1) n_{\text{eff}}(1) = 1, so if N^* \leq 1, n = 1 works.

(2) If \rho = 0, n_{\text{eff}} = n, so we need n \geq N^*.

(3) Suppose 0 < \rho < 1/N^*. Set n_{\text{eff}} = N^* and solve:

\frac{n}{1+(n-1)\rho} = N^*
\iff n = N^*(1 + (n-1)\rho)
\iff n(1 - N^*\rho) = N^*(1-\rho)
\iff n = \frac{N^*(1-\rho)}{1-N^*\rho}.

Since 1 - N^*\rho > 0 and N^* > 1, the right-hand side is positive. We verify n \geq 1:

n - 1 = \frac{N^*(1-\rho) - (1-N^*\rho)}{1-N^*\rho} = \frac{N^* - 1}{1 - N^*\rho} \geq 0.

Thus n_{\min} = \lceil N^*(1-\rho)/(1-N^*\rho) \rceil.

(4) If \rho \geq 1/N^*, then 1 - N^*\rho \leq 0. Since n_{\text{eff}}(n) \to 1/\rho as n \to \infty, the maximum achievable effective depth is 1/\rho \leq N^*. Thus no finite n attains n_{\text{eff}} \geq N^*. ∎

Corollary D.4 (Exponential blow-up near the decorrelation threshold). As \rho \to (1/N^*)^-, n_{\min} \to \infty.

Proof. The denominator 1 - N^*\rho \to 0^+ while the numerator N^*(1-\rho) \to N^* - 1 > 0. ∎

Corollary D.5 (Correction of the earlier formula). The previously proposed formula

d_V \geq \left\lceil \log_{1/\epsilon}(1/\delta) \right\rceil (1+\rho)

is incorrect in two respects. First, it multiplies by 1+\rho, which is not the correct inversion of the effective-depth relation. Second, it never produces an infeasibility condition; the correct formula shows that for \rho \geq 1/N^*, no finite depth suffices.

Proof. At \rho = 1, the previous formula gives d_V \geq 2\lceil N^* \rceil, predicting that additional layers always help. The correct analysis (Theorem D.3, case 4) shows that at \rho = 1, n_{\text{eff}} = 1 for all n, so no finite depth attains the target. The two formulas disagree qualitatively. ∎

D.4 Numerical illustration

Take \epsilon = 10^{-2} (per-layer error 1%), \delta = 10^{-6} (target system error 1 in a million). Then N^* = \ln(10^6)/\ln(10^2) = 6\ln 10 / 2 \ln 10 = 3.

\rho n_{\min} (correct) d_V (earlier formula)
0.0 3 3
0.1 4 4
0.2 5 4
0.3 8 4
1/3 \approx 0.333 \infty (infeasible) 4
0.5 infeasible 5
1.0 infeasible 6

The earlier formula systematically understates the required depth as \rho grows, and it fails to signal infeasibility at \rho \geq 1/N^*.

