Appendix E. Risk Functional: Boundedness, Monotonicity, Limits

Definition E.1 (Risk functional). For domain i, define

z_i(t) = \alpha \ln\!\left(\frac{C_i + \epsilon_C}{V_i + \epsilon_V}\right) + \beta \ln(D_i + \epsilon_D) + \gamma \ln(\mathcal{A}_i + \epsilon_A) - \eta \ln(H_i K_i + \epsilon_H),

\Pr(\text{failure})_i(t) = \sigma_{\text{log}}(z_i(t)) = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-z_i(t)}}.

Proposition E.2 (Boundedness). For all C, V, D, \mathcal{A}, H, K \geq 0 and all \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \eta > 0,

0 < \Pr(\text{failure}) < 1.

Proof. The logistic function \sigma_{\text{log}}: \mathbb{R} \to (0,1) is strictly increasing and bounded. z_i is finite whenever the arguments of the logarithms are positive, which they are by the floors. ∎

Proposition E.3 (Monotonicity).

1. \partial \Pr(\text{failure})/\partial C > 0.
2. \partial \Pr(\text{failure})/\partial V < 0.
3. \partial \Pr(\text{failure})/\partial D > 0.
4. \partial \Pr(\text{failure})/\partial \mathcal{A} > 0.
5. \partial \Pr(\text{failure})/\partial H < 0.
6. \partial \Pr(\text{failure})/\partial K < 0.

Proof. Since \sigma_{\text{log}}'(z) > 0, it suffices to check the signs of the partial derivatives of z_i. For (1): \partial z/\partial C = \alpha/(C + \epsilon_C) > 0. For (2): \partial z/\partial V = -\alpha/(V+\epsilon_V) < 0. For (3): \partial z/\partial D = \beta/(D+\epsilon_D) > 0. For (4): \partial z/\partial \mathcal{A} = \gamma/(\mathcal{A}+\epsilon_A) > 0. For (5): \partial z/\partial H = -\eta/(HK + \epsilon_H) \cdot K = -\eta K/(HK+\epsilon_H) < 0. For (6): symmetric. ∎

Proposition E.4 (Limits).

1. \lim_{C \to \infty} \Pr(\text{failure}) = 1 if V, D, \mathcal{A}, H, K are held fixed.
2. \lim_{V \to \infty} \Pr(\text{failure}) = 0 if C, D, \mathcal{A}, H, K are held fixed.
3. \lim_{H \to 0} \Pr(\text{failure}) = 1 if the other arguments are held fixed and \eta > 0.
4. \lim_{H \to 1, K \to 1} \Pr(\text{failure}) = \sigma_{\text{log}}(z^*) < 1 where z^* excludes the control term; perfect control does not guarantee zero failure probability.

Proof. (1) As C \to \infty, z \to +\infty, so \sigma_{\text{log}}(z) \to 1. (2) As V \to \infty, the first term diverges to -\infty, so z \to -\infty. (3) As H \to 0, -\eta \ln(HK+\epsilon_H) \to +\infty when \eta > 0, so z \to +\infty. (4) When H = K = 1, the control term is -\eta \ln(1 + \epsilon_H), a finite negative number, so z^* is finite and \sigma_{\text{log}}(z^*) < 1. ∎

Remark E.5 (Why perfect control does not drive failure to zero). Residual failure probability arises from unmodeled factors, implementation errors, and the finite reliability of containment. This is why \epsilon_H > 0 is not merely numerical but substantive.

Proposition E.6 (Log-odds decomposition). The log-odds of failure is a weighted sum of log-ratios:

\ln\frac{\Pr(\text{failure})}{1 - \Pr(\text{failure})} = \alpha \ln\frac{C + \epsilon_C}{V + \epsilon_V} + \beta \ln(D + \epsilon_D) + \gamma \ln(\mathcal{A} + \epsilon_A) - \eta \ln(HK + \epsilon_H).

Proof. Invert the logistic: \operatorname{logit}(p) = \ln(p/(1-p)). Apply to p = \sigma_{\text{log}}(z). ∎

