Appendix F. Verification Debt: Well-Posedness, Dynamics, Repayment

Definition F.1 (Verification debt). For t \geq t_0,

B_V(t) = \underbrace{\int_{t_0}^{t} \max(0, C(s) - V(s))\, e^{-\lambda(t-s)}\, ds}_{D(t)} - \underbrace{\int_{t_0}^{t} V^{\text{retro}}(s)\, ds}_{R(t)},

with \lambda > 0 the depreciation rate and V^{\text{retro}} \geq 0 the retroactive verification effort.

Proposition F.2 (Well-posedness). If C, V, V^{\text{retro}} \in L^1([t_0, T]), then B_V(t) is finite and continuous on [t_0, T].

Proof. |D(t)| \leq e^{\lambda(t-t_0)} \int_{t_0}^t |C - V| ds \leq e^{\lambda(T-t_0)} \|C-V\|_{L^1} < \infty. |R(t)| \leq \|V^{\text{retro}}\|_{L^1} < \infty. Both integrals are continuous in t by dominated convergence. ∎

Proposition F.3 (Differential form). B_V is absolutely continuous with

\frac{dB_V}{dt} = \max(0, C(t) - V(t)) - \lambda D(t) - V^{\text{retro}}(t),

where D(t) = B_V(t) + R(t) is the gross debt.

Proof. Differentiate D using Leibniz's rule:

\frac{dD}{dt} = \max(0, C(t)-V(t)) \cdot e^{-\lambda \cdot 0} + \int_{t_0}^t \max(0,C-V)(-\lambda) e^{-\lambda(t-s)} ds = \max(0,C-V) - \lambda D.

Differentiate R: dR/dt = V^{\text{retro}}. Since B_V = D - R, the result follows. ∎

Proposition F.4 (Zero-debt steady state). Suppose C(t) \leq V(t) and V^{\text{retro}}(t) = 0 for all t \geq t_1. Then B_V(t) \to 0 as t \to \infty at rate \lambda.

Proof. For t \geq t_1, \max(0, C-V) = 0, so D(t) = D(t_1) e^{-\lambda(t-t_1)}. Since R is constant for t \geq t_1, B_V(t) = D(t_1) e^{-\lambda(t-t_1)} - R(t_1) \to -R(t_1). If R(t_1) = 0, then B_V \to 0. If R(t_1) > 0, then B_V \to -R(t_1) < 0, which corresponds to over-verification (a surplus). For the non-negative form, replace B_V by \max(0, B_V). ∎

Proposition F.5 (Debt is monotone under sustained capability–verification gap). If C(t) - V(t) > 0 and V^{\text{retro}}(t) = 0 for all t \in [t_0, T], then B_V(t) is strictly increasing on [t_0, T].

Proof. dD/dt = C - V - \lambda D. Suppose D(t_1) \leq 0. Then dD/dt|_{t_1} = C - V - \lambda D > 0, so D increases. If D > 0, dD/dt = C - V - \lambda D. If C - V > \lambda D, D increases. If C - V \leq \lambda D, D decreases toward the equilibrium D^* = (C-V)/\lambda. In either case D is bounded below by 0 after a finite time if D(t_0) \geq 0. For strict monotonicity of B_V = D - R with R = 0, we need dD/dt > 0. This holds whenever D < (C-V)/\lambda. For D(t_0) = 0, dD/dt = C - V > 0, so B_V increases initially. It may later approach the equilibrium and become flat, but not decrease. ∎

Proposition F.6 (Repayment feasibility). Suppose B_V(t_1) > 0. The debt is fully repaid by time t_2 if

\int_{t_1}^{t_2} V^{\text{retro}}(s)\, ds \geq D(t_1).

Proof. B_V(t_2) = D(t_2) - R(t_2) \leq D(t_1) - R(t_2) since D is non-increasing when \max(0,C-V) = 0 and R is non-decreasing. So B_V(t_2) \leq 0 if R(t_2) \geq D(t_1). ∎

Remark F.7 (Repayment window). For empirical work, the repayment window [t_1, t_2] should be specified ex ante (e.g., 30 days for mathematical claims, 1 year for biomedical claims) to avoid post-hoc fitting. See Appendix M.

