Appendix G. Stateful Deployment Threshold: Invariants and Monotonicity

Definition G.1 (Stateful deployment rule). Define

\text{Deploy}(t) = \begin{cases} 1, & V(t) \geq V_{\min}(C, \mathcal{A}, B_V) \text{ and } H(t) \geq H_{\min} \text{ and } K(t) \geq K_{\min}, \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}, \end{cases}

with V_{\min} non-decreasing in C and \mathcal{A}, and non-decreasing in B_V.

Proposition G.2 (Debt gating). If B_V(t) > 0, then there exists a threshold \bar{B}_V such that deployment is blocked whenever B_V > \bar{B}_V, even if V(t) is large.

Proof. By definition, V_{\min} is non-decreasing in B_V. For large enough B_V, V_{\min} > V(t) for any finite V(t), so the first condition fails. ∎

Proposition G.3 (Capability monotonicity of the gate). For fixed V, H, K, B_V, an increase in C cannot enable deployment (it can only disable it or leave it unchanged), because V_{\min}(C, \cdot, \cdot) is non-decreasing in C.

Proof. If C_1 < C_2, then V_{\min}(C_1, \mathcal{A}, B_V) \leq V_{\min}(C_2, \mathcal{A}, B_V). So \text{Deploy}(t) \mid C_2 \leq \text{Deploy}(t) \mid C_1. ∎

Proposition G.4 (Invariant preservation under the design principle). Under the normative principle

\text{Capability expansion} \Rightarrow \text{Verification expansion} \Rightarrow \text{Deployment expansion},

the set \{(C,V,H,K,B_V) : \text{Deploy} = 1\} is invariant under any trajectory that satisfies the deployment condition at all times.

Proof. The rule is a state feedback law. The design principle constrains the admissible trajectory class to those where V and H, K are expanded before D is expanded. Within that class, \text{Deploy} = 1 is preserved by the feedback. ∎

