Appendix I. Institutional Adaptation Dynamics: Linearization and Stability

Definition I.1 (Adaptive governance dynamics). Let R(t) denote the state of the institutional rule set. We model

\frac{dR}{dt} = g(C, V, I, R),

with g Lipschitz in R and continuous in (C, V, I).

Definition I.2 (Institutional adaptation capacity). I(t) = \sigma(t) \ln 2 / T^{\text{adapt}}(t).

Proposition I.3 (Linearization at equilibrium). Let (C^*, V^*, I^*, R^*) be an equilibrium of the joint system with g(C^*, V^*, I^*, R^*) = 0. Linearize:

\delta \dot{R} = g_C \delta C + g_V \delta V + g_I \delta I + g_R \delta R.

If g_R < 0, the equilibrium is locally stable with time constant \tau_R = 1/|g_R|.

Proof. Standard linearization. ∎

Proposition I.4 (Critical adaptation threshold). Suppose the capability input grows at rate \gamma_C: C(t) = C_0 e^{\gamma_C t}. The equilibrium rule set R^*(C) satisfies g(C, V, I, R^*) = 0. If the rule set is to track capability with bounded error, we require

\gamma_C \leq |g_R| \cdot \frac{\partial R^*}{\partial C} \cdot \frac{C_0}{R_0}.

If this inequality fails, G_{CI} grows without bound.

Proof. Differentiate g(C, V, I, R^*(C)) = 0 with respect to time along the trajectory. Apply the implicit function theorem. ∎

Corollary I.5 (Clay Institute as a slow institution). Under Clay rules, T^{\text{adapt}} \geq 2 years (the mandatory waiting period). If capability in mathematics advances on a timescale of days, the adaptation differential A_{CI} = d\ln C/dt - d\ln I/dt is necessarily large and positive. This is not a failure of the Clay Institute; it is a structural property of its rules.

Proof. I = \sigma \ln 2 / T^{\text{adapt}} \leq \ln 2 / (2 \text{ yr}) \approx 0.35 \text{ yr}^{-1}. Capability in the episode advanced from \sim 0 to a claimed Millennium solution in 88 hours, giving d\ln C/dt \approx \ln(1000)/88 \text{ h} \approx 0.078 \text{ h}^{-1} \approx 685 \text{ yr}^{-1}. The ratio A_{CI} \approx 685 is enormous. ∎

