Appendix J. Goodhart Formalization: Principal–Agent Model

Definition J.1 (Principal–agent setup). Let G_{CV} be the true Capability–Verification Gap. Let \hat{G} be an observable proxy used as a gating metric. The agent (a lab) chooses an action a \in \mathcal{A} that affects both the true state and the proxy:

G_{CV} = G(a), \qquad \hat{G} = \hat{G}(a) + \xi,

with \xi noise. The principal sets a threshold \hat{G} \leq \bar{G} for deployment.

Definition J.2 (Goodhart gap). The Goodhart gap is

\Gamma = \sup_{a : \hat{G}(a) \leq \bar{G}} G(a) - \inf_{a : \hat{G}(a) \leq \bar{G}} G(a).

A large \Gamma means the proxy can be gamed to satisfy the gate while the true gap remains high.

Proposition J.3 (Goodhart condition). If \hat{G} and G are not perfectly aligned — i.e., there exists a, a' with \hat{G}(a) = \hat{G}(a') but G(a) \neq G(a') — then \Gamma > 0.

Proof. Immediate from the definition. ∎

Proposition J.4 (Mitigation by multiple metrics). Suppose the principal uses k proxies \hat{G}_1, \ldots, \hat{G}_k and requires \hat{G}_j \leq \bar{G}_j for all j. Then

\Gamma_k \leq \min_{j} \Gamma_j

where \Gamma_j is the Goodhart gap for metric j alone.

Proof. The feasible set \{a : \hat{G}_j(a) \leq \bar{G}_j \ \forall j\} \subseteq \{a : \hat{G}_j(a) \leq \bar{G}_j\} for each j. The sup of G over a subset is \leq the sup over the superset. Similarly for the inf. ∎

Corollary J.5. Adversarial audits of the verification process itself — i.e., independent evaluation of whether V is actually being measured — reduce \Gamma by tightening the feasible set.

