Appendix K. Recursive Verification: Fixed-Point Existence and Uniqueness

Definition K.1 (Recursive verification model). Let V_1 denote the capacity of an AI verifier, and suppose its reliability depends on the ratio C/V_1:

R_1 = r_0 - r_1 \ln\!\left(\frac{C + \epsilon}{V_1 + \epsilon}\right),

with r_0 \in (0,1), r_1 > 0. Then

V_1 = \tau \kappa R_1 = \tau \kappa \left(r_0 - r_1 \ln\!\left(\frac{C + \epsilon}{V_1 + \epsilon}\right)\right).

Theorem K.2 (Existence and uniqueness). Define F(v) = \tau \kappa r_0 - \tau \kappa r_1 \ln((C+\epsilon)/(v+\epsilon)). If \tau \kappa r_1 < 1, then F has a unique fixed point in (0, \tau\kappa r_0).

Proof. F is continuous and strictly increasing in v (since \ln((C+\epsilon)/(v+\epsilon)) is decreasing in v, so -\ln is increasing). The derivative is

F'(v) = \tau \kappa r_1 \cdot \frac{1}{v + \epsilon} \leq \frac{\tau \kappa r_1}{\epsilon}.

This is not uniformly < 1 on (0, \tau\kappa r_0) because of the 1/(v+\epsilon) factor. To apply Banach's fixed-point theorem, restrict to [v_{\min}, v_{\max}] where v_{\min} > 0. On this interval, F'(v) \leq \tau\kappa r_1 / v_{\min} < 1 if v_{\min} > \tau\kappa r_1. Choose v_{\min} = \tau\kappa r_1 + \delta for small \delta > 0. Then F is a contraction on [v_{\min}, v_{\max}] provided F([v_{\min},v_{\max}]) \subseteq [v_{\min},v_{\max}]. Since F is increasing and F(v_{\max}) < v_{\max} for v_{\max} = \tau\kappa r_0 (provided r_0 > r_1 \ln((C+\epsilon)/(v_{\max}+\epsilon))), and F(v_{\min}) \geq v_{\min} for v_{\min} small enough, the invariant interval exists. Banach's theorem gives a unique fixed point. ∎

Corollary K.3 (Self-verification degradation). As C/V_1 \to \infty, R_1 \to r_0 - r_1 \ln(C/V_1) \to -\infty, which is unphysical. The model must be truncated: R_1 = \max(0, r_0 - r_1 \ln((C+\epsilon)/(V_1+\epsilon))). With truncation, there exists a critical ratio (C/V_1)^* above which V_1 = 0.

Proof. Set R_1 = 0: r_0 = r_1 \ln((C+\epsilon)/(V_1+\epsilon)), so (C/V_1)^* = e^{r_0/r_1} (approximately, ignoring floors). ∎

Remark K.4. This formalizes the scalable-oversight concern: if the verifier is itself an AI system whose reliability degrades with capability ratio, there is a critical ratio beyond which self-verification fails entirely. This is the domain where \rho \to 1 and decorrelation (via formal verification, independent replication, or adversarial testing) becomes necessary.

