Positive values indicate a positive gap (verification shortfall); negative values indicate a verification surplus (for the forced theorem).

L.8 Verification debt

During the 17-hour window between announcement and Lean publication, V \approx 0 and C \approx 0.01136 proofs/hour. With \lambda = 0.01 \text{ h}^{-1}:

B_V(17 \text{ h}) \approx \int_0^{17} 0.01136 \, e^{-0.01(17-s)} ds = 0.01136 \cdot \frac{1 - e^{-0.17}}{0.01} \approx 0.01136 \cdot 15.65 \approx 0.178 \text{ proof-equivalents}.

The Lean formalization provides repayment at rate V^{\text{retro}} \approx 0.0175 proofs/hour over 17 hours, giving R \approx 0.298 proof-equivalents, which exceeds the accumulated debt. Thus B_V becomes negative (a surplus) if we do not clip it. With clipping at zero, the debt is fully repaid.

L.9 Uncertainty

The estimates R_1 \approx 0.95, R_2 \approx 0.99, \rho_{12} \approx 0.5, \kappa \approx 0.3, w \approx 0.5 are judgmental. A full treatment would propagate uncertainty via Monte Carlo. Preliminarily, G_{CV}^{\text{overall}} \in [0.1, 0.5] at 50% confidence and [-1.2, 4.9] at 90% confidence, given the sensitivity in Table L.1.

