Appendix N. Estimation of the Correlation Parameter \rho

\rho is not directly observable. We propose four proxies and a calibration procedure.

N.1 Proxies

1. Data overlap (Jaccard). Let D_j be the training corpus of layer j. Then

\rho_{\text{data}}(j,k) = \frac{|D_j \cap D_k|}{|D_j \cup D_k|}.

2. Organizational overlap. \rho_{\text{org}}(j,k) = 1 if same lab, 0.5 if same funder or parent company, 0 if fully independent.
3. Model family distance. If layers use the same base model, \rho_{\text{model}} = 1; if different architectures trained on similar data, \rho_{\text{model}} \approx 0.5; if entirely different approaches, \rho_{\text{model}} \approx 0.
4. Shared failure modes. Given a test set of known-bad results, measure the fraction where layers j and k both fail:

\hat{\rho}_{\text{fail}}(j,k) = \frac{P(F_j \cap F_k) - P(F_j)P(F_k)}{\sqrt{P(F_j)(1-P(F_j)) P(F_k)(1-P(F_k))}}.

N.2 Composite estimate

\hat{\rho}(j,k) = w_1 \rho_{\text{data}} + w_2 \rho_{\text{org}} + w_3 \rho_{\text{model}} + w_4 \hat{\rho}_{\text{fail}},

with weights w_1 + \cdots + w_4 = 1, chosen by cross-validation on held-out failure data.

N.3 Calibration for Navier–Stokes

Pair \rho_{\text{data}} \rho_{\text{org}} \rho_{\text{model}} \hat{\rho}_{\text{fail}} \hat{\rho}
V_1–C 0.9 1.0 1.0 0.8 0.8
